{\b0 \cf1 \f3 \ul0 \strike0 \i0 \fs24 \sa0 \sb0 
 A solution of 5-amino-2-chloropyridine (4.54 g, 35 mmol), ethyl glyoxylate (Fluka, 50% in toluene, (7.24 g, 35 mmol), 18 mL of 95% ethanol, and 22 mL of concentrated HCl was allowed to stand at 25 \u176\'3fC for 1 h in a hydrogenation bottle. Catalyst (550 mg of 5% Pt on carbon) was added, and the mixture was hydrogenated on a Parr low-pressure reaction apparatus charged initially at 50 psi until no further absorption of hydrogen was evident (2 h). Distilled water (25 mL) was added, and the mixture was heated on a water bath to dissolve the white precipitate that formed during the reaction. Removal of the catalyst by filtration of the warm mixture and evaporation of the filtrate gave the glycine hydrochloride as a light tan solid. Concentrated HCl was added, and the mixture was heated at 100 \u176\'3fC for 20 min and then cooled to 25 \u176\'3fC (during this treatment the solid did not dissolve) and allowed to stand for 5 days. Collection of the solid by vacuum filtration gave 5.87 g of tan powder. Concentration of the filtrate gave an additional 0.33 g of product with the same melting point (total yield 80%). 
